From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] server: a hook removed while it is being called is removed once

A hook removed while its chain is in use (some thread is calling it) stays in
the chain, marked dead (proc 0), until the chain is released; remove_hook
already took its threads' hook counts down. The server then took them down
again three ways: a second UnhookWindowsHookEx of the same handle succeeded
(on Windows it fails with ERROR_INVALID_HOOK_HANDLE), the owner thread's end
(remove_thread_hooks) removed the dead hook once more, and a thread leaving
the desktop subtracted it too (add_desktop_hook_count walked dead hooks; a
joining thread counted them). A count below zero tripped the assertion in
add_queue_hook_count, which ended the machine's wineserver: every Windows
program of every user and every service. TightVNC's 32-bit hookldr.exe
unhooking and ending while its global hooks were being called did exactly
that on each viewer disconnect -- the root cause behind 9628's clamp, which
stays as a guard (one program's hooks never end everyone's session) and now
only speaks up for a real imbalance.

Dead hooks are skipped by remove_hook, by add_desktop_hook_count, and the
remove_hook request answers STATUS_INVALID_HANDLE for one.

Gate: test/hookcount-gate.sh (A: unhook twice inside the hook; B: the owner
thread unhooks and ends while the hook is called; C: another thread changes
desktop meanwhile). Mutant SG_MUTANT_HOOK_REMOVE_TWICE.
---
--- a/server/hook.c
+++ b/server/hook.c
@@ -338,6 +338,14 @@
     int global = hook->table == desktop->global_hooks;
     struct thread *thread;
 
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_HOOK_REMOVE_TWICE
+    /* Stained Glass OS (9655): a hook removed while its chain was in use stays
+     * in the chain, dead (proc 0), until the chain is released; its threads'
+     * counts were taken down then. Removing it again (a second
+     * UnhookWindowsHookEx, its owner thread ending) took them down twice --
+     * below zero, which ended the machine's server (TightVNC's hookldr.exe). */
+    if (!hook->proc) return;
+#endif
     if (!global)
         add_queue_hook_count( hook->thread, hook->index, -1 );
     else LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY( thread, &desktop->threads, struct thread, desktop_entry )
@@ -365,6 +373,11 @@
     {
         LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY( hook, &table->hooks[index], struct hook, chain )
         {
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_HOOK_REMOVE_TWICE
+            /* a dead hook (9655) is no longer counted: a thread joining the
+             * desktop must not count it, nor one leaving take it off again */
+            if (!hook->proc) continue;
+#endif
             if (!run_hook_in_thread( hook, thread )) continue;
             add_queue_hook_count( thread, hook->index, count );
         }
@@ -587,6 +600,14 @@
             return;
         }
     }
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_HOOK_REMOVE_TWICE
+    /* as on Windows, a hook already removed is an invalid handle (9655) */
+    if (!hook->proc)
+    {
+        set_error( STATUS_INVALID_HANDLE );
+        return;
+    }
+#endif
 
     remove_hook( hook );
 }
